{
  "note": "The systems the New Machine assistant offers to download (docs/VIRTUALIZATION.md 4.1 and 3.9, \"The Linux download cards\"). Read by the app (apps/machines/catalog.js, bundled) and by the admin helper as root (server/src/vm/vmcatalog.c, from /usr/lib/lordos/assets/machines/systems.json): JS names an entry and an architecture, never a URL. An entry names a release, not a file: 'dir' is the maker's folder for it, 'sums' the maker's own SHA-256 list in that folder (or 'sumsPattern', the list's name to look for in the folder's listing when the name changes per build), and 'file' the image's name as an extended regular expression; the newest matching name in the list is taken, and the download must have the SHA-256 the list gives for it, or it is thrown away. 'fileDir' is where the image itself is fetched when it isn't 'dir' (a mirror redirector). 'approxMb' is only what the card says before the list is read; 'maxMb' is the most a download may be. Before any hash in a list is trusted, the list's OpenPGP signature is checked (vmcatalog.c, pkg/pgp.c): 'signature' is how the maker signs it ('detached', a file named 'name' in the same folder, or 'clearsigned', the list itself), 'keys' the maker's key file, pinned in the package (packaging/keys/machines, installed in /usr/lib/lordos/assets/machines/keys), and 'fingerprints' the primary keys of that file that may sign; a list that isn't signed, or whose signature doesn't verify with them, is refused. 'keySource' says where the key was fetched, 'fingerprintChecked' where its fingerprint was compared, over https, with the one the maker publishes. 'hashSource' says where each hash comes from; 'checked' is when the entry was last tried. An ISO whose maker signs no list (Android, docs/VIRTUALIZATION.md 4.2) is 'pinned' instead: one file, its address and the SHA-256 checked against a download, pinned in this signed file; 'machine' is what its card asks for whatever the profile (vcpus, memoryMb, diskGb, graphics), 'notice' the key of a line the assistant shows under it (machines.dl.notice.<key>, with 'maker' and 'imageDate'), 'status' why it is what it is. 'unattended' says how the card installs without questions (vmunattend.h): the answer file's kind, the installer's kernel and initrd in the ISO for each architecture, the kernel command line ({label}: the ISO's volume label), and 'image', another entry whose image that install boots instead (a server or network installer), marked 'hidden' so it has no card. Order is the cards' order.",
